all right let's spend a few minutes  today talking about a new blockchain  project that's doing some interesting  things in their space called mone this  video was sponsored by mone we also  interviewed mone co-founder Martin era  vets last week which you can check out  in our video feed so let's start with  some background just to get some context  about what mone is doing mone is dealing  with what is called the peer-to-peer  economy and when I say peer-to-peer I'm  talking about transactions or activities  between people who interact directly  with one another and this is in contrast  to a centralized model so peer-to-peer  would be like you selling something to  someone you know without first selling  it to a third party who then sells it to  the buyer or without even a third party  kind of being a middleman in that  transaction and there's many situations  that seem peer-to-peer like when you buy  something on eBay when you buy something  directly from another person or not from  eBay itself but eBay acts as the  intermediary it's not really  peer-to-peer in that strict sense and in  many situations there are benefits to  having a middleman and using a  centralized model when you're using  Airbnb to rent something somewhere to  stay or you're using Craigslist to buy a  service those are some examples of when  a middleman or intermediary can be  useful but there are cost to doing  business through intermediaries one is  you forfeit a certain amount of your  privacy many companies that facilitate  transactions are collecting data about  you another is that there can be a lack  of transparency we don't know how a lot  of these intermediaries are operating we  don't know if everything they're doing  is completely fair to the users or how  much information they collect about us  or any number of things that a user  might benefit from knowing which the  centralized platform may not want you to  know about another reality is the cost  obviously when you use a lot of these  services some take a flat fee some take  a percentage of the transaction some are  in other ways making money off of the  service that they provide which in most  cases is a cost that gets passed on to  the user in one way or another even if  it's not totally obvious how that's  one type of attempt to decentralize  these models and fix these issues has  been to implement public block chains  like Bitcoin or aetherium based  infrastructures but those solutions also  have some inherent inefficiencies for  these types of uses they require every  node in the blockchain network around  the world to process everyone's commands  this paradigm still requires users to  interact with that public blockchain in  the same way they would interact with  other online services the actual  computation and management of data is  left to somebody else  Monay sees peer-to-peer activities  facilitated through centralized third  parties and public block chains to be  paradoxical and not actually truly  peer-to-peer because users aren't just  purely interacting with one another  they're still dealing with these  downsides as a result of intermediaries  what Monet is trying to put in place as  a solution as an alternative to these  client-server models is a system in  which people interact and work and carry  out transactions with one another in a  more actually authentically peer-to-peer  manner this would be done through what  they call mobile ad hoc block chains  basically these would be temporary  miniature block chains where people's  actual mobile devices are the nodes on  the network the network is only as big  as the number of people interacting and  the nodes are only connected for as long  as the users are wanting to interact and  the small block chain that's established  between the nodes the mobile devices of  the users themselves is essentially  playing the role that a central server  played in the traditional model users  don't have to trust a third party they  don't have to delegate work to a third  party as is the case in a centralized  model and the users are only connected  to who they need to be connected to  based on their geographical location  users don't have to stay online to  support other users as is the case in a  large decentralized network which Monet  sees as inefficient for these  peer-to-peer applications and where  Monet is a platform and company comes in  is build  maintaining open source software kits  that let developers build the blockchain  consensus technology into their apps  it's a really fascinating project  conceptually several mechanisms coming  into play in ways that we haven't seen  before also make it more interesting and  as far as blockchain on mobile devices  and how that's being implemented I'm  looking forward to seeing how it follows  up how they follow up and continuing to  discuss it this video is sort of part 1  of 2 video overviews I'll be doing about  Monet so stay tuned for part 2 where I  will go more in depth about how the  platform works and again check out my  interview with Monet's co-founder martin  era vets you can find out even more  about monet by going to monet dot  network i've put a link below underneath  this video
 

